#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Paints the three levels of the instance maps, the dungeons and raids (Doc/guides/map-style.md; Saneroki,
2026-10-21: strict maps of the layout, not free concept art). Every instance has one frame, its box in the world,
that all three levels share, all three seen straight from above (Saneroki, 2026-10-22), so they lie over each other
exactly and every hall, place and pin is in the same spot in each.

  close    the terrain, pixel-exact: squares of one terrain tile at 2048 pixels (dev/tools/map-squares) over the
           instance's terrain, its map's tiles with the indoor maps of the buildings they place laid over them, as
           the game's minimap draws them (dev/tools/indoor-art's parts, placed as Navigation places them); the words
           of close.toml with the brief's colours and light, the check against the game's water (up to three seeds)
           and the colour pass. MOST_SQUARES at most, over the instance's middle; --whole-close: over all of it.
  middle   a map in the manner of the game's own, laid exactly over the terrain, drawn in pencil and ink on
           parchment, straight from above (levels.toml, [middle]): image 1 the floor's map art in the frame, its
           title banner painted over (where the game has none, the terrain plan, another place's map for its manner
           only), image 2 the terrain plan, image 3 the parchment in the tone of the game map's land. Floor by floor.
  far      a photograph of the place taken straight down from high above, its layout kept with a little leeway
           (levels.toml, [far]), in one picture, painted from noise, the Fun ControlNet Union keeping its layout:
           image 1 its plan, blurred and toned down (far_colours); image 2 a piece of the close level; the control
           the plan's clearest edges (edges_of). (Not the middle level's drawing: it was copied. The sharp terrain
           as image 1 came back as the game's own.)

Each middle and far painting is checked twice and painted again with the next seed until one passes, TRIES at most:
  outlines  how well its edges agree with the terrain plan's (their normalised correlation at OUTLINE pixels): a
            middle level against how well the game's own map agrees with it (MIDDLE of that), a far one at least
            FAR and at most COPY (more is its image 1 come back as it is);
  looks     the vision model (qwen3vl-look) answers in JSON: writing in it; a pencil map on tinted parchment seen
            from above (middle); no panels, seen straight down, of the brief's kind (far; it calls every photograph
            straight down a map, so that answer counts for nothing).

Usage:
  dev/tools/map-layers inputs [--instances MAPS] [--tables DIR --instance-files DIR --mapart DIR] [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers paint [--levels close,middle,far] [--whole-close] [--only NAMES] [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers join [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers contact [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers status [--levels close,middle,far] [--out DIR]

  inputs    the instances' frames, terrain plans and the game's map art, into the run's state.json: --instances
            36,209,3065; the game's folders (--tables the client tables as CSV, --instance-files the instances'
            terrain, --mapart the map art) are needed the first time, then the state.json remembers them
  paint     the levels in order (the far level takes a piece of the close one, else of the terrain plan), what is
            missing only; --only instance-36,...; --whole-close: the close level over the whole frame
  join      every instance's close squares joined again, cut along the line where neighbours differ least
  contact   contact.jpg: a row per instance: its terrain plan, the game's map, close, middle and far
  status    its first line: "<made> of <all> paintings made", of the levels asked (a job's own share)

A subject of the run's state.json that is no instance (a zone of an earlier run) is passed over by every command.

It writes, in the run's folder (default assets/generated/map-layers, never committed), per instance <name>
(instance-<map> and its floors instance-<map>-<floor>):
  <name>/ground.png, art.png          the terrain plan and the game's map art in the frame
  <name>/close/<i>-<j>.png, close.png the close squares, painted, and joined
  <name>/parchment.png                the middle level's image 3
  <name>/far-colours.png              the far level's image 1; far-edges.png its control; far-close.png its image 2
  <name>/middle-<try>.png, far-<try>.png and checks.json (each try's outline agreement and the vision model's answer)
  state.json                          the paths and subjects
Needs Python with Pillow and NumPy, and ComfyUI; the instances' terrain extracted from the game into a folder of
its own (wdt/, adt/, adt-obj0/, minimap/, wmo/, wmo-minimap/, tiles.jsonl), as dev/tools/indoor-art reads it.
